    <br/>

    <table cellpadding="2" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" align="center">
    <tr>
        <td bgcolor="#bbbbbb" width="1%" nowrap align="center">
            &nbsp;<font color="#ffffff"><b>Description</b></font>&nbsp;
        </td>
        <td>&nbsp;</td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
    <tr>
        <td id="descriptionArea">
            <p>Admin interface uses weak authentication model - TCP (or port-based), which increases the risk of unauthorized access - Critical Description: The service currently uses Dropwizard to provide administrative functionalities. The admin related tasks or APIs use TCP-based (port-based) authentication. Using TCP-based authentication is generally considered weak, as access control relies entirely on access to the port. Every process on the machine that has access to the port will be able to call the Admin API. This makes it hard to implement granular access control. An SSRF vulnerability could allow access to the privileged admin port from localhost, bypassing any network firewall or external restrictions and enabling the attacker to execute internal-only endpoints via the service. A misconfigured local app or service could access those ports and perform malicious actions. A Malicious operator with limited shell access might be able to connect to the API, violating the principle of least privilege. Impact: Unauthorized access can lead to the leakage of sensitive data, data manipulation, and service disruptions.</p>
            <br/>
        </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    <br/>

    <table cellpadding="2" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%" align="center">
    <tr>
